boethius-1.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: boethius-1.logic.r1 and boethius-1.logic.r1.3 have "servants_vicious → servants_burden" and "servants_honest → virtue_others" and "virtue_others → ¬servants_thine"; boethius-1.logic.r1.2 has "servants_vicious → servants_burden" and "servants_vicious → ¬servants_thine" and "servants_honest → virtue_others" and "virtue_others → ¬servants_thine"; they differ when only "the servants behave viciously", "the servants are a ruinous burden to thy house and dangerous to their master" and "the servants (their worth) can be counted in the sum of thy possessions" are true, which the first allows
boethius-1.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered yes, which keeps boethius-1.logic.r1 and boethius-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "the servants behave viciously", "the servants are a ruinous burden to thy house and dangerous to their master" and "the servants (their worth) can be counted in the sum of thy possessions" are true and "the servants are honest" and "the servants' goodness is other men's virtue" are false. Is S2, "Why, if they behave viciously, they are a ruinous burden to thy house, and exceeding dangerous to their own master; while if they are honest, how canst thou count other men's virtue in the sum of thy possessions?", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
boethius-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: boethius-1.logic.r1 and boethius-1.logic.r1.2 have "servants_vicious ∨ servants_honest" and "¬servants_thine → ¬possessions_thine" and "pleasing_without ∧ ¬precious_from_counting → ¬beauty_thine" and "¬beauty_thine → ¬ground_grief_joy"; boethius-1.logic.r1.3 has "servants_vicious ∨ servants_honest" and "servants_burden → ¬servants_thine" and "¬servants_thine → ¬possessions_thine" and "pleasing_without ∧ ¬precious_from_counting ∧ ¬possessions_thine → ¬beauty_thine" and "¬beauty_thine → ¬ground_grief_joy"; they differ when only "the servants behave viciously", "the servants are a ruinous burden to thy house and dangerous to their master", "the servants are honest", "the servants (their worth) can be counted in the sum of thy possessions", "the things thou reckonest among thy possessions are really thine", "their beauty is anything to thee", "thou hast reason to grieve for their loss or find joy in their possession", "they would have been no less pleasing in themselves though never included among thy possessions" and "they derive their preciousness from being counted in thy riches" are true, which the first allows
boethius-1.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: boethius-1.logic.r1 and boethius-1.logic.r1.2 have "¬possessions_thine"; boethius-1.logic.r1.3 has "¬ground_grief_joy"; they differ when only "thou hast reason to grieve for their loss or find joy in their possession" is true, which the first allows
boethius-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: boethius-1.logic.r1 and boethius-1.logic.r1.2 put it at S3, "From all which 'tis plainly proved that not one of these things which thou reckonest in the number of thy possessions is really thine."; boethius-1.logic.r1.3 at S4, "And if there is in them no beauty to be desired, why shouldst thou either grieve for their loss or find joy in their continued possession?"
boethius-1.logic.r1: argue/answered: M: answered yes, which keeps boethius-1.logic.r1 and boethius-1.logic.r1.2, to: Is the text's main conclusion S3, "From all which 'tis plainly proved that not one of these things which thou reckonest in the number of thy possessions is really thine.", rather than S4, "And if there is in them no beauty to be desired, why shouldst thou either grieve for their loss or find joy in their continued possession?"?
boethius-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: boethius-1.logic.r1.2 and boethius-1.logic.r1.3 take for granted "if the servants are a ruinous burden to thy house and dangerous to their master, then it is not so that the servants (their worth) can be counted in the sum of thy possessions"; boethius-1.logic.r1 does not
boethius-1.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps boethius-1.logic.r1.2 and boethius-1.logic.r1.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if the servants are a ruinous burden to thy house and dangerous to their master, then it is not so that the servants (their worth) can be counted in the sum of thy possessions"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
boethius-1.logic.r1: argue/verdicts: valid as boethius-1.logic.r1.2, boethius-1.logic.r1.3 read it, not as the others do: which reading is right decides the verdict [in 1 of 2 runs]
